  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nI propose a multi-resolution spectral optimization approach combined with physics-informed neural networks. This differs significantly from the parent strategy that relies on temperature annealing and grid-based searches. The key advantages are:\n1. Faster convergence through spectral acceleration\n2. Better handling of periodic components in step functions\n3. Multiple physics-informed layers to model different shift behaviors\n4. Progressive resolution refinement that maintains feasibility constraints throughout\n\nThe approach tackles the non-convex nature by:\n- Representing step functions as superpositions of periodic basis functions\n- Using multi-resolution analysis to capture large and small structures\n- Injecting problem-specific physics into the optimization landscape\n- Employing a hierarchical neighborhood exploration strategy\n\nThe main innovation is replacing the grid-based search with a spectral-adaptive method that maintains the unitarity constraint (sum h_i * dx = 1) exactly through basis function selection.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h(x) as a linear combination of Fourier-Bessel basis functions:\nh(x) = \u03c3\u2080 + \u03a3_{k=1}^{D} \u03c3_k * J\u2080(a_k * \u03c0 * x / W)\nwhere W is the domain width, J\u2080 is the Bessel function of the first kind, and \u03c3 are coefficients.\n\nConstraints are enforced through:\n1. Linear transformation ensuring \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n2. \u03c3 parameters bounded in [-1, 1] range\n3. Bessel filter ensuring boundedness\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k))dx\n\nOver a grid of N=280 points, but parameterized in D-dimensional space (D \u2248 14-28)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Parent validation:\n   - Validate initial_h_values shape (n,) with n=280\n   - Compute affine transform preserving integral constraint\n\n2. Initialization:\n   - Create dual-layer spectral representation (low/high frequencies)\n   - Compute initial spectrum using fast Bessel transform\n   - Ensure basis functions satisfy orthogonality/maxflatness\n   - Set initial coefficients with uniform distribution, projected to feasible set\n\n3. Core optimization with adaptive resolution:\n   - Temperature-driven spectrum annealing\n   - Multi-scale gradient projection\n   - Physics-constrained conjugate gradient steps\n   - Feasibility restoration via projection to simplex\n\n4. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Energy-based scoring with logarithmic penalty for violations\n   - Hierarchical comparison criteria:\n     \u2022 Lower C5 values first\n     \u2022 Feasibility prioritization\n     \u2022 Spectral consistency check\n\n5. Adaptive resolution management:\n   - Coefficient space dimension doubling when:\n     \u2022 Local improvement plateau reaches 0.002\n     \u2022 Global energy exceeds threshold\n   - Basis function upsampling maintaining:\n     \u2022 Orthogonality properties\n     \u2022 Zero-average condition\n     \u2022 Translation invariance\n\n6. Feasibility restoration:\n   - Non-negativity enforced via exponential transformation\n   - Boundedness guaranteed by clamping at coefficients level\n   - Integral constraint maintained by tracepreserving map\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\n1. Basis selection:\n   - Default: Fourier-Bessel hybrid with frequency cutoff K=16\n   - Variants: Slepian sequences (PEN), Gabor atoms (GAB), Chebyshev (CHEB)\n\n2. Optimization parameters:\n   - Primary: Annealing schedule [30, 150, 400, ...] with log-spaced temperatures\n   - Secondary: Energy decay factor \u03c1 in [0.95, 0.999]\n   - Tertiary: Projection threshold \u03b5 = 1e-3 \n\n3. Dimensionality control:\n   - Start with D=14 (low resolution)\n   - Double D (D=28) when:\n     \u2022 Minimum correlation observed < 0.3\n     \u2022 Relative improvement < 0.0005\n   - Maximum D=56 (final resolution)\n\n4. Restart mechanisms:\n   1. Geometric restart: \u03c3_new = \u03b1\u03c3 + (1-\u03b1)\u03c3\u0303\n   2. Basis restart: switch to orthogonal complement space\n   3. Canonical restart: \u03c3_new = (-1)^t \u03c3\u0303 + \u03b4t\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n\n- Level 0 (D=14): ~40s\n- Level 1 (D=28): ~500s\n- Level 2 (D=56): ~600s\n\nTotal ~700s reserve with 1100s budget\n\nStop conditions:\n- Budget exceeded (return best candidate)\n- No improvement 1000 iterations\n- Feature saturation detected via gradient analysis\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - Project h onto L1-ball with volume matching\n   - Verify \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (tolerance 1e-8)\n   - Ensure h_i in [0,1] (box constraints)\n   - Check energy expression against brute-force calculation\n\n2. Scoring:\n   - Bottoms-up evaluation using correlate()\n   - Warm-up passes for basis function adaptation\n   - Final verification using alternative discretizer\n\n3. Output format:\n   - 1D NumPy array of length n for h_values\n   - C5_bound as float (scalar from compute_c5)\n   - n_points as integer (always 280)\n\nImplementation details will include helper functions for:\n- Fast orthogonal spectral transforms\n- Custom \u03c3-constrained gradient implementations\n- Dynamic basis adaptation routines\n- Energy landscape smoothing filters\n\nThe approach maintains both the parent's constraint handling and explores significantly different mathematical representation, providing a fundamental re-engineering of the optimization process rather than incremental improvement.",
